# Deploying {{project_name}} with Docker (single user)

`nexura-runtime deploy --target docker` wrote `Dockerfile`, `.dockerignore` and this file.
It did not build an image or start a container.

## What runs

The image installs this project and runs `nexura-runtime dev --container`: the bundled Nexura
Studio with this project's handlers and workflows (from `[tool.nexura]` in `pyproject.toml`),
on port {{port}}. The REST service that `studio-web` and the TypeScript client call is not
started: the open-source service has no authentication, so it must stay on the host's
loopback interface (`nexura-runtime dev` on your machine).

Data (run history, checkpoints) is kept in `/app/.nexura`; mount a volume there to keep it.

## Build and run

The image installs Nexura {{nexura_version}} from PyPI. To build against unreleased Nexura
changes instead, build the wheels in a Nexura checkout (`uv build --all-packages`), copy the
`.whl` files into `./wheels/` here, and add `--build-arg PIP_FIND_LINKS=/app/wheels`:

```bash
docker build -t {{project_name}} .
export NEXURA_STUDIO_TOKEN="$(python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))')"
docker run --rm -p 127.0.0.1:{{port}}:{{port}} -e NEXURA_STUDIO_TOKEN \
    -v {{project_name}}-data:/app/.nexura {{project_name}}
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:{{port}}/#token=<your token>`. Pass model and embedding keys the same
way (`-e OPENAI_API_KEY`); never bake them into the image or commit a `.env` file.

## Security caveats

From Nexura's `SECURITY_MODEL.md`:

- The open-source edition is **single user, this machine only**. For a team or a public
  endpoint use the hosted or enterprise edition. There are no accounts, roles or tenant
  isolation; whoever has the token has full authority.
- Inside a container Studio must listen on `0.0.0.0` so the port can be published. It then
  refuses to start without `NEXURA_STUDIO_TOKEN`, and every API request must carry the token.
  The token is one shared secret, not a per-user login.
- Publish the port on `127.0.0.1` only (`-p 127.0.0.1:{{port}}:{{port}}`). Never publish it on
  `0.0.0.0` or put it behind a public proxy, and never add
  `--insecure-allow-unauthenticated-network`.
- The image runs as an unprivileged user (uid 10001). Workflow handlers are your own Python
  code and run with that user's rights inside the container.
- Nexura has not had an external security review.
